content.text:
Right — `issueImessageTokens` *is* called again on every renewal. The build loop just isn't in that path.

Look at the shape of `createCloudClients` (`packages/imessage/src/auth.ts`):

```ts
export async function createCloudClients(projectId, projectSecret) {
  let tokenData = await cloud.issueImessageTokens(...)   // ① once
  const records = []

  const renewal = createTokenRenewal({
    refresh: async () => {
      tokenData = await cloud.issueImessageTokens(...)   // ② re-issued — but only reassigns the variable
      if (tokenData.type === "dedicated") syncPhones(tokenData)  // ③ walks `records` only
    },
  })

  for (const [instanceId, token] of Object.entries(dedicated.auth)) {  // ④ the build loop
    records.push({ entry: { phone, client: createGrpcClient({ ... }) }, instanceId })
  }
  return records.map((r) => r.entry)                     // ⑤ returned once
}
```

④ is in the **function body**, not in `refresh`. `refresh` is a closure with exactly two jobs: swap the `tokenData` variable, and rewrite `entry.phone` on records that already exist. So ② hands back a payload that genuinely contains the new line's token and number — and then nothing reads the new key.

That's deliberate, not an oversight. The per-RPC token function is `async () => { await renewal.refreshIfNeeded(); return tokenData.auth[instanceId] ?? token }` — it closes over the mutable `tokenData` and looks up *its own* instanceId at call time. The whole point (comment at `auth.ts:63`) was that a rotated token reaches the existing channel without tearing down and rebuilding gRPC connections. Rebuilding clients on refresh is precisely what the design was avoiding; growing the set was never part of the job.

One nuance that matters for the fix: if `refresh` did push a new entry into the returned array, **outbound would start working** — `clientForPhone` and `randomPhone` read the array at call time, so they'd see it. **Inbound still wouldn't**, because `messages()` already ran and `mergeStreams` froze its worker set from `clients.map(...)` at construction. That asymmetry is why the issue lists the `mergeStreams` late-addition change as a separate step rather than folding it into the `refresh` diff.

